# `crawl` — the only construct that reaches SCANS without also reaching API, and the one this pane
# **draws and does not build** (`M228` `C`, `D1238`).
#
# Until this round a crawl was invisible in the whole UI: `outline.ts` said in as many words that
# *`crawl` declarations are the SCANS door's own root and are not here*, while the sidebar badge
# counted them. So this file's row used to read **2** and list **1**, and only on this door — the
# door the crawl is the entire point of.
#
# Select either declaration below and the pane draws its name, its principals, its seeds and its
# excludes, read-only, **saying why**: there is no `buildCrawl` and `insertIntoSource` splices a
# `test` and nothing else, so a crawl is edited in the file. ▶ and `✕` are absent rather than
# drawn held, because a crawl has no index for `--only` or `replaceInSource` to name.

# **`seed traffic` reads the traffic of THIS FILE**, which is why the test is here and not
# elsewhere: traffic accumulates across the whole file and is collected only when a crawl in that
# file declares the seed, and the crawl runs after every test in its file rather than where it is
# typed. Put the crawl beside the requests you want walked.
@catalogue
test "the pages the crawl below will re-issue as a stranger"
  api GET /
  expect status equals 200
  api GET /signin
  expect status equals 200

@catalogue
crawl "everything this file touched, walked again as a stranger"
  seed traffic
  exclude "/signin"
  expect response has no serious security violations

# A second crawl, with **two** seeds and two expects, so the band's seed list is not a list of one
# and the read-only rows are not a row of one.
#
# **It said `seed openapi "/openapi.json"` until 2026-09-22 and could never have run**: the
# storefront serves `/`, `/signin`, `/items`, `/baskets`, `/orders`, `/orders/checkout`,
# `/login` and `/health`, and answers **404** to that path. The header's own excuse — *"it is here
# to be authored against"* — was the wrong call for a playground: a corpus that cannot run teaches
# the door's picture and nothing about its verdict.
#
# `seed spider` takes the service ident **optionally** (`parser.ts:3037`), so `seed spider "/"`
# needs no `web` target and no extra config; the caps are indented sub-clauses. The note that used
# to sit here claiming otherwise was wrong about the grammar, which is how the unreachable seed got
# chosen in the first place.
@discovery
crawl "the documented surface, and whatever this file touched"
  seed spider "/"
    max pages 5
    max depth 2
  seed traffic
  exclude "/logout"
  # **The floors are measured, not chosen.** Against this storefront `critical` is *vacuous* on a
  # crawled response — 3 rules, 0 applicable, and the runtime refuses it by name: *"this assertion
  # had no power to fail"* (SPEC §9.10, `D285`). `serious` has applicable rules and passes;
  # `moderate` is where the storefront's two real gaps live. So the hard assertion is set where it
  # can fail and does not, and the soft one is set where it finds something.
  #
  # **The `check` line is RED on purpose and is the only red in this corpus that is a finding.**
  # The spider walks 5 URLs and the crawl reports 3 `moderate` violations — `sec/x-frame-options`
  # and `sec/nosniff-missing` on the storefront's own pages. That is the door doing its job, and a
  # playground whose scan finds nothing would teach the picture and not the verdict. `check` is the
  # continue-on-failure form: the crawl keeps walking and the test still ends red.
  expect response has no serious security violations
  check response has no moderate security violations
